module std where

-- =========================================================================
-- List ⇄ native-array spine bridges (representation-substrate Phase 3).
--
-- `List a` is `$opaque { name:"List", pure: μX. Nil | Cons head:a tail:X }`:
-- in production the runtime stores a JS array (the repr); the pure cons-spine
-- (`{$tag:"Cons", _0:h, _1:t}` / `{$tag:"Nil"}`) is the semantics. These two
-- bridges cross that boundary — they ARE the `Reprable JsRuntime (List a)`
-- lift/lower the design (R5) identified (fenel/std/dispatch-instances.fenel).
--
--   listToArray : List a -> List a   the LIFT  — fold the cons-spine into the
--                                    native array (the PERF direction). Bottoms
--                                    out at the `concat`/`[]`/`[h]` array repr.
--   arrayToList : List a -> List a   the LOWER — fold the native array into the
--                                    cons-spine (the REASONING direction). Walks
--                                    the array with the `head`/`drop` natives.
--
-- BOTH sides are typed `List a -> List a`: `List` is ONE type with two reprs
-- (cons-spine vs JS array), so the native rep IS `List` (its array view) — the
-- `native = List` of the instance, the Bool-degenerate parallel (native type
-- name == pure type name) but with DISTINCT runtime values (cons-spine ≠ array,
-- unlike Bool's coinciding JS booleans). The opaque boundary is real here.
--
-- ELEMENT-TRANSPARENT (the §1.2 invariant that makes the instance well-defined):
-- each element `h` passes through UNTOUCHED — the spine is the only thing
-- re-encoded. A `List<Nat>` round-trips with its Peano elements byte-identical;
-- lifting/lowering the ELEMENTS is the element type's own `Reprable`, composed by
-- the caller, NOT this bridge. (Compositional element-repr — re-encoding each
-- element while crossing — is the storage-target's concern, Phase 5; the
-- JsRuntime spine bridge does not.) This is the analog of "natToNumber counts the
-- spine": the spine is the structure, the payload rides along.
--
-- These never run on the hot path — production list ops stay on the native array
-- fast branch (the std-ffi.ts list natives — concat/head/drop/map/… — are the
-- ledgered opaque-repr PERF BRANCH of this instance; design/ts-exceptions-ledger.md
-- B4). The bridges exist for the law/reasoning side: the pure cons-spine the
-- type system reasons over, materialized on small samples.

@label "List to array"
@description "Fold a pure cons-spine List into its native array repr (the Reprable JsRuntime (List a) LIFT). listToArray Nil = []; listToArray (Cons h t) = concat [h] (listToArray t). Element-transparent — each head rides through untouched. Structural recursion on the spine ⇒ terminates; below the totality checker's List rungs (Unknown), total over the intended List domain."
@tags "list" "array" "repr" "bridge"
listToArray : (xs: List a) -> List a
listToArray xs = case xs of
  Nil -> []
  Cons h t -> concat [h] (listToArray t)

@label "Array to list"
@description "Fold a native array into the pure cons-spine List (the Reprable JsRuntime (List a) LOWER). arrayToList walks the array with the head/drop natives: empty ⇒ Nil; else Cons (head) (arrayToList (drop 1)). Element-transparent — each element rides through untouched. Recurses on the strictly-shorter `drop 1` tail ⇒ terminates over the finite array domain."
@tags "list" "array" "repr" "bridge"
arrayToList : (arr: List a) -> List a
arrayToList arr = case head arr of
  Nothing -> Nil
  Just h -> Cons h (arrayToList (drop 1 arr))
